## Eureka Server Setup

```java
// eureka-server/
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableEurekaServer
public class EurekaServerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(EurekaServerApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

```yaml
# eureka-server/application.yml
server:
  port: 8761

eureka:
  instance:
    hostname: localhost
  client:
    registerWithEureka: false    # Don't register itself
    fetchRegistry: false         # Don't fetch its own registry
  server:
    enableSelfPreservation: false  # Evict dead instances faster (dev/test)
    evictionIntervalTimerInMs: 5000
```

## Eureka Client (Each Microservice)

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class OrderServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(OrderServiceApplication.class, args);
    }

    // RestTemplate with load balancing:
    @Bean
    @LoadBalanced  // Intercept calls and resolve service names via Eureka
    public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
        return new RestTemplate();
    }

    // WebClient with load balancing:
    @Bean
    @LoadBalanced
    public WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder() {
        return WebClient.builder();
    }
}
```

```yaml
# order-service/application.yml
spring:
  application:
    name: ORDER-SERVICE  # This is the service name in the registry

eureka:
  client:
    serviceUrl:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
    registryFetchIntervalSeconds: 30   # How often to refresh registry cache
    instanceInfoReplicationIntervalSeconds: 30
  instance:
    preferIpAddress: true              # Use IP instead of hostname
    leaseRenewalIntervalInSeconds: 10  # Heartbeat every 10 seconds
    leaseExpirationDurationInSeconds: 30  # Evicted if no heartbeat for 30s
```

## Calling Another Service by Name

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final RestTemplate restTemplate;  // @LoadBalanced injected
    private final WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder;

    // RestTemplate — service name as hostname:
    public UserInfo getUserInfo(Long userId) {
        // 'USER-SERVICE' is resolved to a real instance by LoadBalancer:
        return restTemplate.getForObject(
            "http://USER-SERVICE/api/users/{id}",
            UserInfo.class,
            userId
        );
    }

    // WebClient — reactive:
    public Mono<InventoryStatus> getInventory(String productId) {
        return webClientBuilder.build()
            .get()
            .uri("http://INVENTORY-SERVICE/api/inventory/{productId}", productId)
            .retrieve()
            .bodyToMono(InventoryStatus.class);
    }

    // Using Feign client (declarative HTTP client):
    // @FeignClient(name = "USER-SERVICE")
    // public interface UserServiceClient {
    //     @GetMapping("/api/users/{id}")
    //     UserInfo getUser(@PathVariable Long id);
    // }
}
```

## Registry Lifecycle

```
1. SERVICE STARTUP:
   OrderService starts → registers with Eureka:
   {
     "app": "ORDER-SERVICE",
     "ipAddr": "10.0.0.5",
     "port": 8080,
     "status": "UP",
     "healthCheckUrl": "http://10.0.0.5:8080/actuator/health"
   }

2. HEARTBEAT:
   Every 30s (default), OrderService sends heartbeat
   Eureka resets expiry timer

3. CLIENT CACHE:
   Other services fetch registry every 30s
   Client has LOCAL CACHE — doesn't call Eureka on every request
   Consequence: takes ~60s (2 × 30s) to see new instance

4. INSTANCE DIES:
   No heartbeat for 90s (default) → evicted from registry
   Next cache refresh → callers stop routing to dead instance

5. SELF-PRESERVATION MODE:
   If >85% of instances miss heartbeat, Eureka suspects network issue
   Stops evicting (assumes it has connectivity problem, not clients)
   Disable in dev: enableSelfPreservation: false
```

## Kubernetes Alternative

```yaml
# K8s Service — DNS-based discovery (no Eureka needed)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: order-service       # DNS name within cluster
  namespace: production
spec:
  selector:
    app: order-service
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
---
# Other services call: http://order-service.production.svc.cluster.local
# Or short: http://order-service  (within same namespace)
# K8s kube-proxy handles load balancing automatically
```

## Production Reality

In Kubernetes, Eureka is largely redundant — K8s has built-in service discovery via DNS and Services. Use Eureka when deploying to traditional VMs or non-K8s environments. In K8s, just use Spring Cloud Kubernetes which integrates with K8s service discovery, ConfigMaps, and Secrets natively. The `@LoadBalanced` + service name pattern works the same regardless of the discovery mechanism underneath.
